2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,17,87,284,220,18,88,284,481,89,59,60,285,264,1555,382,785,738,315,3501,4930,87,7358,22669,15087,429,26553,198,78045,21,87,284,481,88,284,481,19,89,59,60,285,2441,1555,382,9885,279,9210,1948,1493,5128,11,304,12348,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[2x = 3y = -z\\]is a line.\n\nThe set of points $(x,y,z)$ that satisfy\n\\[6x = -y = -4z\\]is another line.\n\nFind the angle between these lines, in degrees.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"f045b09de53842fd533792ede3e908086bf157ae91bc9b45e66d907607a07bd7"},"seconds":32.09600591659546,"seed":157206495144120526,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the angle between the two lines given by the equations \\(2x = 3y = -z\\) and \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), we first need to express these lines in parametric form.\n\nStarting with the first line \\(2x = 3y = -z\\), we can set \\(2x = 3y = -z = t\\). Then, we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{t}{2}, \\quad y = \\frac{t}{3}, \\quad z = -t.\\]\nSo, the parametric form of the first line is:\n\\[\\left( \\frac{t}{2}, \\frac{t}{3}, -t \\right) = t \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right).\\]\nThe direction vector of the first line is \\(\\mathbf{d_1} = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right)\\).\n\nNext, for the second line \\(6x = -y = -4z\\), we can set \\(6x = -y = -4z = s\\). Then, we have:\n\\[x = \\frac{s}{6}, \\quad y = -s, \\quad z = \\frac{s}{4}.\\]\nSo, the parametric form of the second line is:\n\\[\\left( \\frac{s}{6}, -s, \\frac{s}{4} \\right) = s \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right).\\]\nThe direction vector of the second line is \\(\\mathbf{d_2} = \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right)\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) between the two lines is the same as the angle between their direction vectors \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\). The cosine of the angle between two vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) is given by:\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}| |\\mathbf{b}|}.\\]\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\\[\\mathbf{d_1} \\cdot \\mathbf{d_2} = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, -1 \\right) \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{6}, -1, \\frac{1}{4} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (-1) + (-1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{4}{12} - \\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1 - 4 - 3}{12} = -\\frac{6}{12} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\\]\n\nNext, we compute the magnitudes of \\(\\mathbf{d_1}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{d_2}\\):\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_1}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{9} + 1} = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{36} + \\frac{4}{36} + \\frac{36}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} = \\frac{7}{6},\\]\n\\[|\\mathbf{d_2}| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\right)^2 + (-1)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} + 1 + \\frac{1}{16}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{36} + \\frac{36}{36} + \\frac{9}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{46}{36}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{18}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{414}}{18} = \\frac{\\sqrt{23 \\cdot 18}}{18} = \\frac{\\sqrt{23} \\cdot \\sqrt{18}}{18} = \\frac{\\sqrt{23} \\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}}{18} = \\frac{\\sqrt{46}}{6}.\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\cos \\theta\\):\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7}{6} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{6}} = \\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}}{\\frac{7\\sqrt{23}}{36}} = -\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{36}{7\\sqrt{23}} = -\\frac{18}{7\\sqrt{23}} = -\\frac{18\\sqrt{23}}{161}.\\]\nThe angle \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\\[\\theta = \\cos^{-1} \\left( -\\frac{1}{2} \\right) = 120^\\circ.\\]\nThus, the angle between the two lines is \\(\\boxed{120}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{120}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"precalculus:515"}
